text:
Taylor: FYI hit a weird duplicate-charge in the sampleapp when token rotation coincided with a 429. minimal repro attached in the branch. anyone else see this pattern?

Riley: can you paste the short call trace? might be related to how we reuse request objects on retry.

Taylor: heres the sequence in logs: original POST /v1/charge (token A) -> auth refresh rotates to token B mid-flight -> upstream responds 429 to original socket -> SDK issues retry with token B but the client didn't set an idempotency header on the retried envelope. server accepted both.

Sam: that smells like a race where the SDK mutates headers in-place and the retry path re-sends the mutated object without regenerating computed headers. we had a similar bug in go for websockets once.

Helena: quick note: our recommended pattern is to generate an `Idempotency-Key` per logical operation, not per transport send. if the SDK is generating it per transport and then loses it on retry, duplicates will happen.

Taylor: agreed. current node behavior: we build headers when the request is originally created and then reuse the same header map on retry; when auth refresh returns a new token we replace Authorization but forget to copy over Idempotency-Key into the fresh request envelope used by the retry engine.

Riley: parity question: what do other SDKs do?

Sam: python and go create the final HTTP message at send-time (so they always compose Authorization + Idempotency-Key together). ruby similar. node is an outlier.

Helena: proposed fixes: 
- SDK: always compose a fresh header map at retry-time. clone any computed headers (Idempotency-Key) or move generation upstream to the request factory.
- Server: opt-in `X-RW-RESUME` header returned on 429 with a short-lived token. if client retries with `X-RW-RESUME` server will check last seen operation id and dedupe.
- Backoff: switch to full jitter to avoid thundering herds: `wait = uniform(0, base * 2^attempt)` and add small random delta to token refresh timing.

Taylor: we should also add a tiny CI harness to sampleapp that simulates token rotation + transient 429s and asserts no duplicate side-effects (email/charge). i'll open a test-first branch.

Riley: small API UX nit: expose an explicit `idempotency` flag on SDK call builders so customers can opt into auto-generation, e.g. `client.requests.create({..., idempotency: 'auto'})` so we don't surprise SDK users who intentionally want multiple sends.

Sam: note on long-running streams: resume-token makes sense when the operation is resumable. for opaque side-effecting endpoints (charge/send-email) resume-token should be limited to dedup lookup only. no resuming of body.

Helena: security note: resume tokens must be short lived and bound to account + operation hash to avoid replay by third parties. also rotate server keys for token signing periodically.

Taylor: here's a tiny snippet from the failing test harness (node):
```js
// simulate token rotation + 429
await fakeAuth.rotateToken();
server.on('request', (req, res) => {
  if (Math.random() < 0.5) {
    res.writeHead(429, {'X-RW-RESUME': 'tok-123'});
    return res.end();
  }
  // process charge once
});
```

Riley: thanks. i'll pick up the node SDK change to compose headers at retry-time. will include unit tests with fake timers and a deterministic random seed for jitter.

Sam: i'll spec the resume token format and a minimal HMAC scheme for signing. will also add server-side dedupe TTL of ~90s for Hosted. Dedicated can extend.

Helena: filed DX card: https://redwood.atlassian.net/browse/DX-1299 and linked to the sampleapp PR when available.

